Specification ImportOfx

Description Reading a QFX or OFX download, and deciding for each transaction in
\ it whether it is new, already present, or the arrival of something
\ entered by hand.
Details
\ Importing the same file twice must change nothing. That is the single
\ requirement everything in this file serves, because over twenty years
\ of downloads it is certain to happen by accident.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What the file says
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Entity OfxStatement
Description The header of one statement in the file, naming the account it is for.
| Attribute    | Type        | Default    | Notes                             |
| BankId       | Text        | none       | Routing number; absent on cards   |
| OfxAccountId | Text        |            | The account number the bank uses  |
| OfxAcctType  | OfxAcctType | CHECKING   |                                   |
| Currency     | Text        | USD        |                                   |
| StartDate    | Date        | 2024-01-01 | Of the period downloaded          |
| EndDate      | Date        | 2024-01-31 |                                   |
| LedgerBal    | Money       | 0.00       | What the bank says the balance is |
| LedgerBalAt  | Date        | 2024-01-31 |                                   |

DataType OfxAcctType
Description The account type OFX reports, which is not the SimpleAccount type.
Examples: EnumerationValues
| Value       | Notes                                   |
| CHECKING    |                                         |
| SAVINGS     |                                         |
| MONEYMRKT   |                                         |
| CREDITLINE  |                                         |
| CREDITCARD  | From a CCACCTFROM block rather than BANKACCTFROM |
| INVESTMENT  | From an INVACCTFROM block               |

Entity OfxTransaction
Description One STMTTRN block.
| Attribute  | Type        | Default    | Notes                              |
| TrnType    | OfxTrnType  | DEBIT      |                                    |
| DatePosted | Date        | 2024-01-01 | From DTPOSTED                      |
| Amount     | Money       | 0.00       | From TRNAMT, signed by the bank    |
| FitId      | FitId       |            | From FITID                         |
| Name       | Text        | none       | From NAME, the payee as the bank has it |
| Memo       | Text        | none       | From MEMO                          |
| CheckNum   | CheckNumber | none       | From CHECKNUM                      |

DataType OfxTrnType
Description The transaction type OFX reports. It is recorded but not relied on:
\ the sign of the amount is what decides direction.
Examples: EnumerationValues
| Value     | Notes                       |
| CREDIT    | Money in                    |
| DEBIT     | Money out                   |
| INT       | Interest earned or charged  |
| DIV       | Dividend                    |
| FEE       | Service charge              |
| SRVCHG    | Service charge              |
| DEP       | Deposit                     |
| ATM       |                             |
| POS       | Point of sale               |
| XFER      | Transfer                    |
| CHECK     | A paper check               |
| PAYMENT   |                             |
| CASH      |                             |
| DIRECTDEP | Direct deposit              |
| DIRECTDEBIT |                           |
| REPEATPMT | A standing order            |
| OTHER     |                             |

Collection OfxTransactions
| DataType       | Minimum | Maximum | Notes                        |
| OfxTransaction | 0       | 100000  | FitId is unique per statement |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Parsing the file
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Scenario Reading an OFX 1 file, which is SGML and not XML
Description Most bank downloads are OFX 1.0.2 with unclosed tags, and a strict
\ XML parser rejects them. The reader closes a tag when the next one
\ opens at the same level, which is what the format intends.
Given the file contains
"""
OFXHEADER:100
DATA:OFXSGML
VERSION:102
SECURITY:NONE
ENCODING:USASCII

<OFX>
<BANKMSGSRSV1><STMTTRNRS><STMTRS>
<CURDEF>USD
<BANKACCTFROM><BANKID>053000196<ACCTID>1234567890<ACCTTYPE>CHECKING</BANKACCTFROM>
<BANKTRANLIST><DTSTART>20240101<DTEND>20240131
<STMTTRN><TRNTYPE>DEBIT<DTPOSTED>20240115120000[-5:EST]<TRNAMT>-45.00<FITID>20240115001<NAME>HARRIS TEETER #231<MEMO>PURCHASE</STMTTRN>
<STMTTRN><TRNTYPE>CHECK<DTPOSTED>20240118<TRNAMT>-1250.00<FITID>20240118002<CHECKNUM>2041<NAME>COUNTY TAX OFFICE</STMTTRN>
<STMTTRN><TRNTYPE>DIRECTDEP<DTPOSTED>20240131<TRNAMT>3000.00<FITID>20240131003<NAME>ACME PAYROLL</STMTTRN>
</BANKTRANLIST>
<LEDGERBAL><BALAMT>1705.00<DTASOF>20240131</LEDGERBAL>
</STMTRS></STMTTRNRS></BANKMSGSRSV1>
</OFX>
"""
Then statement is : OfxStatement Vertical
| BankId       | 053000196  |
| OfxAccountId | 1234567890 |
| OfxAcctType  | CHECKING   |
| Currency     | USD        |
| StartDate    | 2024-01-01 |
| EndDate      | 2024-01-31 |
| LedgerBal    | 1705.00    |
| LedgerBalAt  | 2024-01-31 |
And transactions read are : OfxTransactions
| TrnType   | DatePosted | Amount   | FitId       | Name               | Memo     | CheckNum |
| DEBIT     | 2024-01-15 | -45.00   | 20240115001 | HARRIS TEETER #231 | PURCHASE | none     |
| CHECK     | 2024-01-18 | -1250.00 | 20240118002 | COUNTY TAX OFFICE  | none     | 2041     |
| DIRECTDEP | 2024-01-31 | 3000.00  | 20240131003 | ACME PAYROLL       | none     | none     |

Scenario Reading an OFX 2 file, which is XML
Description Newer downloads are well-formed XML with every tag closed. The same
\ reader handles both, so nothing downstream knows which it was.
Given the file contains
"""
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<?OFX OFXHEADER="200" VERSION="211" SECURITY="NONE"?>
<OFX>
  <CREDITCARDMSGSRSV1><CCSTMTTRNRS><CCSTMTRS>
    <CURDEF>USD</CURDEF>
    <CCACCTFROM><ACCTID>4111111111111111</ACCTID></CCACCTFROM>
    <BANKTRANLIST>
      <DTSTART>20240301</DTSTART><DTEND>20240331</DTEND>
      <STMTTRN>
        <TRNTYPE>DEBIT</TRNTYPE><DTPOSTED>20240305</DTPOSTED>
        <TRNAMT>-89.99</TRNAMT><FITID>CC-20240305-1</FITID>
        <NAME>AMAZON MKTPLACE</NAME>
      </STMTTRN>
    </BANKTRANLIST>
    <LEDGERBAL><BALAMT>-89.99</BALAMT><DTASOF>20240331</DTASOF></LEDGERBAL>
  </CCSTMTRS></CCSTMTTRNRS></CREDITCARDMSGSRSV1>
</OFX>
"""
Then statement is : OfxStatement Vertical
| BankId       | none             |
| OfxAccountId | 4111111111111111 |
| OfxAcctType  | CREDITCARD       |
| Currency     | USD              |
| StartDate    | 2024-03-01       |
| EndDate      | 2024-03-31       |
| LedgerBal    | -89.99           |
| LedgerBalAt  | 2024-03-31       |
And transactions read are : OfxTransactions
| TrnType | DatePosted | Amount | FitId         | Name            | Memo | CheckNum |
| DEBIT   | 2024-03-05 | -89.99 | CC-20240305-1 | AMAZON MKTPLACE | none | none     |

BusinessRule An OFX date is read to the day
Description DTPOSTED may carry a time and a timezone. The time is discarded:
\ a register is kept by date, and keeping the time would make the
\ same transaction sort differently depending on the reader timezone.
Examples: OfxDateParse
| Text                      | Date       | Notes                           |
| 20240115                  | 2024-01-15 | Date only                       |
| 20240115120000            | 2024-01-15 | Time discarded                  |
| 20240115120000.000        | 2024-01-15 | Milliseconds discarded          |
| 20240115120000[-5:EST]    | 2024-01-15 | Timezone discarded              |
| 20240115235959[-5:EST]    | 2024-01-15 | Not rolled into the next day    |
| 20240101000000[+13:NZDT]  | 2024-01-01 | Nor into the previous one       |

Attributes OfxDateParse
| Attribute | Type   | Default    |
| Text      | String |            |
| Date      | Date   | 2024-01-01 |
| Notes     | String | none       |

BusinessRule The amount in an OFX file is used as the transaction amount
Description TRNAMT is signed from the point of view of the account the statement
\ is for, which is the same convention the transactions use. No account
\ type needs a sign flip, including a credit card: a charge is
\ negative in the file and a credit to the card in the book.
Examples: OfxAmountToPosting
| OfxAcctType | TrnAmt  | PostingAmount | Notes                        |
| CHECKING    | -45.00  | -45.00        | Money out of checking        |
| CHECKING    | 3000.00 | 3000.00       | Pay in                       |
| CREDITCARD  | -89.99  | -89.99        | A charge; more is now owed   |
| CREDITCARD  | 300.00  | 300.00        | A payment; less is now owed  |
| SAVINGS     | 1.23    | 1.23          | Interest                     |

Attributes OfxAmountToPosting
| Attribute     | Type        | Default  |
| OfxAcctType   | OfxAcctType | CHECKING |
| TrnAmt        | Money       | 0.00     |
| PostingAmount | Money       | 0.00     |
| Notes         | String      | none     |

Scenario A file that is not OFX at all is reported, not guessed at
Given the file contains
"""
Date,Description,Amount
2024-01-15,HARRIS TEETER,-45.00
"""
Then rejected because : Rejection
| Reason                                                  |
| No OFX header found; this looks like a CSV file         |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Which account a download is for
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Description A download names its account in the bank's own terms -- a routing
\ number, and the number the bank keeps the account under -- and the
\ account itself is where those terms are kept. The account a
\ statement belongs to is the account whose number the statement
\ names.
Details There is no table of mappings. An account carries the name of its
\ bank, that bank's id and its own number, the three of them together;
\ see what is kept about an account in Accounts. Finding the account
\ for a download is reading those off the chart. A second place to
\ record a bank's numbering would be a second thing to keep in step
\ with the first, and the only question a download asks -- which
\ account is this -- is answered by an account or not at all.
\
\ A number is kept as a person writes it and a file gives bare digits,
\ so it is the digits of each that are compared; the rule below says
\ how. The routing numbers have to agree whenever both say anything,
\ because two banks number their accounts independently and over
\ twenty years one of them reaches a number the other has used.
\
\ An account that knows none of this is matched by nothing, and that is
\ the ordinary state of every account brought over from Quicken: a QIF
\ carries no routing number and no account number. The first download
\ into such an account is where they arrive; see what a download
\ teaches an account, below.

Scenario A statement whose number is on no account asks which account it is
Description The first download from an institution names a number that means
\ nothing yet. The import stops and asks, rather than inventing an
\ account.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path            | Type     | Placeholder | BankId | AccountNumber |
| Assets:Checking | Checking | false       | none   | none          |
When statement imported : OfxStatement Vertical
| BankId       | 053000196  |
| OfxAccountId | 1234567890 |
| OfxAcctType  | CHECKING   |
Then asked to choose an account for : UnknownAccount Vertical
| BankId       | 053000196  |
| OfxAccountId | 1234567890 |
| OfxAcctType  | CHECKING   |
| Suggestion   | none       |

Attributes UnknownAccount
Description What the dialog is told about a statement no account claims.
| Attribute    | Type        | Default  |
| BankId       | Text        | none     |
| OfxAccountId | Text        |          |
| OfxAcctType  | OfxAcctType | CHECKING |
| Suggestion   | AccountPath | none     |

Scenario A download whose number is on an account imports without asking
Description Which is every month after the first. The account was told the
\ bank's numbers once and nobody is asked again.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path            | Type     | Placeholder | BankId    | AccountNumber |
| Assets:Checking | Checking | false       | 053000196 | 1234567890    |
When statement imported : OfxStatement Vertical
| BankId       | 053000196  |
| OfxAccountId | 1234567890 |
| OfxAcctType  | CHECKING   |
Then import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
| Asked       | false           |

Attributes ImportTarget
| Attribute   | Type        | Default |
| AccountPath | AccountPath |         |
| Asked       | Boolean     | false   |

BusinessRule When the number in a download is the number on an account
Description Asked of every real account in the chart. One account saying yes is
\ the account it goes to; none, and the import asks. Two is also
\ asked, because a number that names two accounts has named neither.
Details Only the digits of each are compared, because the account's number
\ is kept as somebody wrote it -- 0012-3456, or a card number in
\ groups of four -- and the file gives nothing but digits.
\
\ Equal digits are the ordinary case. Beyond that, the digits of one
\ ending the digits of the other count, which is what lets an account
\ labelled with the last four of a card meet the full number the
\ download carries. Four digits is the floor, the same floor the
\ file-name rule uses in UserInterface: three digits is a month or a
\ sequence number, and matching an account on three digits would put
\ a statement in the wrong account for a living.
\
\ The routing numbers have to agree whenever both are stated. A card
\ statement states none, and an account that has not been told its
\ bank's id states none; neither of those is a disagreement, because
\ nothing was claimed.
Examples: DownloadAccountMatch
| StatementBankId | StatementAccountId | AccountBankId | AccountNumber        | Matches |
| 053000196       | 1234567890         | 053000196     | 1234567890           | true    |
| 053000196       | 1234567890         | none          | 1234567890           | true    |
| none            | 4111111111114455   | 053000196     | ~4111 1111 1111 4455 | true    |
| 053000196       | 0012-3456          | 053000196     | 0012 3456            | true    |
| 053000196       | 4111111111114455   | 053000196     | 4455                 | true    |
| 053000196       | 4111111111114455   | 053000196     | 455                  | false   |
| 053000196       | 1234567890         | 053000196     | 0012-3456            | false   |
| 053000196       | 1234567890         | 021000021     | 1234567890           | false   |
| 053000196       | 1234567890         | 053000196     | none                 | false   |
# The third row is a card: no routing number in the file, one on the account,
# and the number written in groups of four. The second to last is the one the
# routing number is there for -- the same account number at two banks.

Attributes DownloadAccountMatch
| Attribute          | Type    | Default |
| StatementBankId    | Text    | none    |
| StatementAccountId | Text    |         |
| AccountBankId      | Text    | none    |
| AccountNumber      | Text    | none    |
| Matches            | Boolean | false   |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What a download teaches an account
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Description An account that was never told the bank's numbers is told them by
\ the first download confirmed for it. Nothing already there is
\ changed.
Details This is the whole of how the numbers get onto an account without
\ anybody typing them. A history imported from Quicken carries none,
\ so asking once per account is the least that can be asked, and
\ writing down the answer is what makes it once rather than monthly.
\
\ Only what is blank is filled in. A number a person typed is what the
\ account dialog shows them, and a download that disagreed with it
\ would be rewriting a label they chose, in a place they were not
\ looking, on the strength of their having confirmed something else.
\ So a disagreement leaves the account alone and writes a note, and
\ the account dialog is where it is settled.

Scenario The first download confirmed for an account tells it the bank's numbers
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type     | Placeholder | BankId | AccountNumber |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking | false       | none   | none          |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense  | false       | none   | none          |
When statement imported : OfxStatement Vertical
| BankId       | 053000196  |
| OfxAccountId | 1234567890 |
| OfxAcctType  | CHECKING   |
And import target chosen : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
Then account numbering is : AccountNumbering
| Path            | BankId    | AccountNumber |
| Assets:Checking | 053000196 | 1234567890    |
# Asked once. The number the bank uses is on the account now, so the rule
# above places next month's download without anybody being asked again.

Attributes AccountNumbering
Description The two things on an account that a download is matched against.
| Attribute     | Type        | Default |
| Path          | AccountPath |         |
| BankId        | Text        | none    |
| AccountNumber | Text        | none    |

Description The balance the download stated is written on the account, with the
\ date it was the balance on, and kept there.
Details The check against LEDGERBAL above happens once, during the import, and
\ then the number is gone. What it is wanted for is the other three
\ weeks of the month: the register says one thing, the bank's website
\ says another, and the question is always which of the two moved
\ since. Knowing the balance the bank last agreed to, and the day it
\ agreed to it, turns that into arithmetic. Not knowing it turns it
\ into reading a statement line by line.
\
\ So it is one more thing the account knows, like the bank id and the
\ number -- written by the import, not typed -- and the register shows
\ it beside the balance it works out for itself. See the two balances
\ at the top of the register in TransactionRegister.
\
\ A balance older than the one already written is not an answer to
\ anything, so it is not written. Downloads arrive out of order often
\ enough -- a file saved last month and imported today -- and the most
\ recent statement is the only one worth keeping.

Attributes AccountDownloadedBalance
Description The balance a download last stated for one account. Never is a word
\ rather than a date, because an account the program has never
\ downloaded for has no balance to disagree with, which is not the
\ same as one whose bank said zero.
| Attribute  | Type        | Default |
| Path       | AccountPath |         |
| Balance    | Money       | 0.00    |
| BalanceAt  | Text        | never   |

Scenario The balance a download states is written on the account
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type     | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense  | false       |
And import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
When ledger balance checked : LedgerBalanceCheck Vertical
| Account     | Assets:Checking |
| StatedBal   | 1705.00         |
| StatedBalAt | 2024-01-31      |
Then downloaded balances are : AccountDownloadedBalance
| Path            | Balance | BalanceAt  |
| Assets:Checking | 1705.00 | 2024-01-31 |

Scenario An account no download has reached has no balance from the bank
Description The case the word never is for. The register shows what it works
\ out and nothing to compare it against, which is every account in a
\ book imported from Quicken.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type     | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense  | false       |
Then downloaded balances are : AccountDownloadedBalance
| Path            | Balance | BalanceAt |
| Assets:Checking | 0.00    | never     |

Scenario A download older than the balance on the account does not replace it
Description February is imported, then a January file somebody had saved. The
\ account keeps February, because the question it answers is what the
\ bank last agreed to.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type     | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense  | false       |
And import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
When ledger balance checked : LedgerBalanceCheck Vertical
| Account     | Assets:Checking |
| StatedBal   | 1705.00         |
| StatedBalAt | 2024-02-29      |
And ledger balance checked : LedgerBalanceCheck Vertical
| Account     | Assets:Checking |
| StatedBal   | 1900.00         |
| StatedBalAt | 2024-01-31      |
Then downloaded balances are : AccountDownloadedBalance
| Path            | Balance | BalanceAt  |
| Assets:Checking | 1705.00 | 2024-02-29 |
# The January figure is not wrong; it is simply not the latest, and the latest
# is the only one the register has room to show.

Scenario A number already on the account is left as it is and noted
Description The account says one number, the file says another, and the person
\ confirmed that account anyway. Which of the two is right is not
\ something an import can know, so it changes neither and says so.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type     | Placeholder | BankId    | AccountNumber |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking | false       | 053000196 | 0012-3456     |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense  | false       | none      | none          |
When statement imported : OfxStatement Vertical
| BankId       | 053000196  |
| OfxAccountId | 1234567890 |
| OfxAcctType  | CHECKING   |
And import target chosen : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
Then account numbering is : AccountNumbering
| Path            | BankId    | AccountNumber |
| Assets:Checking | 053000196 | 0012-3456     |
And import finishes with : ImportCompletion Vertical CompareOnly
| NoteCount | 1 |

Scenario A bank id arrives for an account that only knew its number
Description The number was typed in the account dialog and the routing number
\ was not, because nobody reads a routing number off a statement to
\ label an account. The download has one, the account has none, and
\ none is not a disagreement.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type     | Placeholder | BankId | AccountNumber |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking | false       | none   | 1234567890    |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense  | false       | none   | none          |
When statement imported : OfxStatement Vertical
| BankId       | 053000196  |
| OfxAccountId | 1234567890 |
| OfxAcctType  | CHECKING   |
Then import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
| Asked       | false           |
And account numbering is : AccountNumbering
| Path            | BankId    | AccountNumber |
| Assets:Checking | 053000196 | 1234567890    |
# Nobody was asked -- the number was enough to place it -- and the routing
# number was still learned, so a second account at a second bank with the
# same number can be told from this one later.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deciding what to do with each transaction
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

DataType ImportDisposition
Description What the import decided about one downloaded transaction.
Details There were five of these once, and one of them was the mistake. A
\ Duplicate did nothing at all and a Matched stamped and cleared the
\ transaction it had found, which made them two answers to one
\ question -- is this transaction already here -- distinguished by how
\ the program had come to know it. Nobody reading an import wants that
\ distinction. What they want to know is that the row is already in
\ the book, and then, separately, whether anything about it did not
\ fit. So there are four dispositions and a note.
Examples: EnumerationValues
| Value     | Notes                                                           |
| New       | Not seen before; a transaction is created                       |
| Duplicate | Already in the book; the one there is cleared and kept, nothing is created |
| Possible  | The same date and amount is there, but the counts disagree; asks |
| Completed | Created, and named on the half of a payment already here, because somebody said the two were the same |

DataType DuplicateNote
Description What did not fit about a duplicate. Written on the transaction in
\ the book, so it is still there next week when somebody wonders why
\ that row is cleared, and shown against the row while the import is
\ being looked at.
Examples: EnumerationValues
| Value           | Notes                                                           |
| None            | The identifier matched and nothing differed                     |
| DetailsDiffer   | The identifier matched and the date, the amount or the payee did not |
| CheckNumberSame | A check number settled it                                       |
| FitIdAdded      | There was no identifier for this source; one was written        |
| PayeeUnmatched  | The date and the amount agreed and the payees did not            |
| Uncleared       | The transaction already there was typed, not downloaded          |

BusinessRule An identifier is only an identifier to the file that gave it
Description A CSV and a QFX describing the same transaction do not agree on what
\ to call it. So an identifier is stored with the name of the source
\ that gave it, and the identifier test applies only where the source
\ matches the import being done. Against any other source the transaction
\ counts as having no identifier at all, and the weaker tests decide.
Details Without this, importing a QFX after a CSV finds no FITID anywhere and
\ calls the whole statement new, duplicating a month of transactions.
\ With a single identifier field instead of one per source it is no
\ better the other way round: stamping the FITID over the CSV reference
\ would make the next CSV import offer every row again.
\
\ So a transaction collects identifiers rather than having one. A transaction
\ imported from a CSV and later recognised in a QFX carries both, and
\ re-importing either file does nothing.
Examples: IdentifierScope
| PostingCarries        | ImportIs | TestOneApplies | Then                                 |
| Nothing               | Ofx      | No               | The weaker tests decide            |
| An Ofx id, the same   | Ofx      | Yes              | Duplicate                          |
| An Ofx id, a different one | Ofx | No               | The weaker tests decide            |
| A Csv id only         | Ofx      | No               | As if it had none; may match       |
| A Csv id only         | Csv      | Yes, if it is the same | Duplicate                    |
| A Csv id and an Ofx id | Ofx     | Yes, on the Ofx one | Duplicate                       |

Attributes IdentifierScope
| Attribute      | Type   | Default |
| PostingCarries | String |         |
| ImportIs       | String |         |
| TestOneApplies | String |         |
| Then           | String |         |

BusinessRule How a downloaded transaction is identified
Description In order. The first test that applies decides.
Details Test one is what makes a second read of the same file do nothing. It
\ is scoped to the account, because two banks may issue the same
\ identifier, and to the source, because a CSV and a QFX do not agree
\ on what to call one transaction. See an identifier is only an
\ identifier to the file that gave it, above.
\
\ Test two is the check number, and it is the only test here allowed
\ any tolerance about the date. A check number is written on the check
\ itself, so the book and the download are naming the same piece of
\ paper -- but only within the book's check-number window, because
\ numbers come round again. It asks for the amount and the payee to
\ agree as well, and it is asked only of a checking account: a card
\ reference is not a check number and nothing else in a book is paid
\ by check.
\
\ Tests three and four are one test counted two ways. The key is the
\ date and the amount, in this account, over the rows and the
\ transactions that tests one and two have not already settled. Where
\ the file has as many of them as the book does, every one is a
\ duplicate. Where it does not, every one is a question: nothing says
\ which row goes with which transaction, and deciding either way
\ doubles a transaction or loses one. See how many rows match how many
\ transactions, in ImportCsv.
\
\ In this account means in this account's register, which is not the
\ same as the transactions this account owns. A transfer entered
\ against the other account names this one as its other side, and is a
\ line here on the strength of that. Counting only the transactions
\ whose SourceAccount is this account makes every transfer in look new,
\ and the money arrives a second time. See a transfer entered from the
\ other account is a duplicate in this one, below.
\ The payee is not part of the key. If it were, the first reconciliation
\ against a history brought over from Quicken would match nothing --
\ Harris Teeter against HARRIS TEETER #231 -- and a month would import
\ twice. So the payee is compared afterwards, and what it decides is
\ the note rather than the disposition. See two payees are compared
\ after the rules and then loosely, below.
\
\ Every other date comparison is to the day. Reaching further is left
\ to a pass of its own, after the import has been accepted, where the
\ question is a different one: pairing a transaction in this register
\ against one in another register, the two halves of a transfer days
\ apart. That pass has a window because the two dates come from two
\ different places. Nothing in it happens while a row is being judged.
\ See proposing transfers.
\
\ Cleared status decides nothing here. It used to: an uncleared
\ transaction was taken to be one somebody typed and was asked about
\ rather than matched, and a cleared one was taken to be a second
\ reading of a download and matched silently. Both are the same
\ transaction arriving again, so both are duplicates now, and the fact
\ that one of them was typed is recorded as a note instead of changing
\ the answer.
Examples: DispositionRule
| Test | Condition                                                             | Disposition |
| 1    | A transaction in this account carries this FITID, from an Ofx import   | Duplicate   |
| 2    | This is a checking account, and a transaction in it has the same check number, amount and payee, dated within the book's check-number window | Duplicate |
| 3    | The rows and the lines of this account's register with this date and this amount are equal in number | Duplicate |
| 4    | There are lines of this account's register with this date and this amount, and they are not equal in number to the rows | Possible |
| 5    | Nothing above applies                                                 | New         |
# A row that is the other half of a payment already in the book is still New
# here. Whether it is that half is asked after the judging, on the amount and
# the date, and answered by a person -- see the rule about a half already in
# the book.

Attributes DispositionRule
| Attribute   | Type              | Default |
| Test        | Integer           | 0       |
| Condition   | String            |         |
| Disposition | ImportDisposition | New     |

BusinessRule What a duplicate does to the transaction already there
Description Nothing is created. The transaction already in the book is the
\ transaction, and the import brings it up to date rather than
\ replacing it: cleared, given the identifier the row carried if it
\ had none for this source, and otherwise left exactly as it is.
Details Keeping the book's own date, payee, category, memo and check number
\ is the point of the whole exercise. A history brought over from
\ Quicken has twenty years of categorising in it and a download knows
\ none of it: the bank's name for a payee is HARRIS TEETER #231 and
\ its category is nothing at all. An import that overwrote what it
\ recognised would undo that work a month at a time.
\
\ Cleared is the exception, because cleared is not something a person
\ chose. It is the bank saying the transaction happened, and the row
\ being read is the bank saying it. So a duplicate clears what it
\ recognised, and a transaction that was typed and never cleared is
\ cleared now -- with a note, because that is the one case where the
\ import has changed something a person could be surprised by.
\
\ The identifier is written for the source that gave it and alongside
\ any the transaction already has, which is what lets the same
\ transaction be recognised by a QFX after a CSV. See an identifier is
\ only an identifier to the file that gave it.
Examples: WhatADuplicateDoes
| Property         | WhatHappens                                              |
| The date         | The book's is kept                                       |
| The payee        | The book's is kept                                       |
| The category     | The book's is kept                                       |
| The memo         | The book's is kept                                       |
| The check number | The book's is kept                                       |
| The amount       | The book's is kept, and is equal in any case             |
| Cleared          | Set to cleared                                           |
| The identifier   | Written, if the transaction had none for this source     |
| The note         | Added to the transaction, where there is one to add      |

Attributes WhatADuplicateDoes
| Attribute    | Type   | Default |
| Property     | String |         |
| WhatHappens  | String |         |

BusinessRule What the note on a duplicate says
Description A duplicate is not always a plain one, and the note is where
\ whatever did not fit is said. It is written on the transaction in
\ the book rather than against the import, because the question it
\ answers -- why is this cleared, why is it spelt like that -- is
\ asked long after the import has been put away.
Details Several can be true of one duplicate: a transaction that was typed,
\ whose payee the download spells differently, and which had no
\ identifier until now. All of them are kept, and each is kept once.
\ A later import that reaches the same transaction and sees the same
\ thing adds nothing, so re-reading a file does not grow a list.
\
\ None of them changes the disposition. Every row below is a duplicate;
\ the note is what a person reads to decide whether to look at it.
Examples: DuplicateNoteRule
| WhatWasSeen                                                           | Note            |
| The identifier matched and the date, amount and payee agree            | None            |
| The identifier matched and the date, the amount or the payee differs   | DetailsDiffer   |
| A check number settled it                                              | CheckNumberSame |
| The transaction had no identifier for this source and now has one      | FitIdAdded      |
| The date and the amount agree and the payees do not                    | PayeeUnmatched  |
| The transaction already there was uncleared                            | Uncleared       |

Attributes DuplicateNoteRule
| Attribute   | Type          | Default |
| WhatWasSeen | String        |         |
| Note        | DuplicateNote | None    |

Attributes TransactionNote
Description A note an import left on a transaction. It is a row rather than a
\ field because several can be true of one transaction, and it is on
\ the transaction rather than against the import because the question
\ it answers is asked long after the import was put away.
| Attribute | Type           | Default |
| Ref       | TransactionRef |         |
| Note      | DuplicateNote  | None    |

BusinessRule Two payees are compared after the rules and then loosely
Description The book says Harris Teeter and the download says HARRIS TEETER
\ #231. They are the same shop, and a comparison that says otherwise
\ puts a note on every row of the first reconciliation against a
\ Quicken history, which is a note on nothing.
Details So the download's payee goes through the payee rules first -- the
\ same rules that rename it when a transaction is created, and the
\ only thing that can know AMZN Mktp US*2X4 is Amazon. If a rule
\ renames it, the renamed name is what is compared. See Payees.
\
\ If no rule applies, the two names are compared loosely: folded to
\ upper case, runs of blanks collapsed, punctuation dropped, and a
\ trailing run of digits or a store number taken off. What is left is
\ compared for equality. It is deliberately not a resemblance score:
\ a number saying two names are eighty per cent alike is not something
\ a person can check or a specification can state.
\
\ A payee that still does not agree does not stop the duplicate. The
\ same date and the same amount in one account is enough to say this is
\ the same transaction; what the payee decides is whether the book's
\ name for it is worth a second look. So the duplicate stands and the
\ note says the payee was not matched.
Examples: PayeeComparison
| InTheBook     | InTheFile          | Agree | Why                                             |
| Harris Teeter | HARRIS TEETER      | Yes   | Case alone                                      |
| Harris Teeter | HARRIS~~TEETER     | Yes   | Runs of blanks collapse                         |
| Harris Teeter | HARRIS TEETER #231 | Yes   | A store number is not part of the name          |
| Joe Coffee    | JOE COFFEE 02      | Yes   | A trailing number comes off                     |
| Shell         | SHELL OIL 57444692 | No    | OIL is not a number; a rule is what joins these |
| Amazon        | AMZN~Mktp~US*2X4   | No    | Nothing but a rule could know this              |
| Harris Teeter | Costco             | No    | Two different shops                             |

Attributes PayeeComparison
| Attribute | Type      | Default |
| InTheBook | PayeeName | none    |
| InTheFile | PayeeName | none    |
| Agree     | Text      | No      |
| Why       | String    | none    |

Scenario Importing the same file twice imports nothing the second time
Description The FITID is stored on the transaction in the statement account, which
\ is what makes this test possible at all.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type     | Placeholder | BankId    | AccountNumber |
