Attributes ImportedTransaction
Description A transaction that also carries the identifier it was imported with.
\ Everything but FitId reads as it does on Transaction itself; the
\ identifier is a fact about the file the row came from, which is why
\ it is here and not on the transaction.
| Attribute     | Type           | Default    |
| Ref           | TransactionRef |            |
| Date          | Date           | 2024-01-01 |
| Payee         | PayeeName      | none       |
| SourceAccount | AccountPath    |            |
| Destination   | AccountPath    | none       |
| Amount        | Money          | 0.00       |
| Cleared       | ClearedStatus  | Uncleared  |
| CheckNo       | CheckNumber    | none       |
| FitId         | FitId          | none       |

Scenario A check entered by hand is cleared when the bank reports it
Description The check was written on the second and reached the bank on the
\ third. One day, so the check number is trusted and the two are the
\ same check.
\ The import does not create a second transaction: it marks the one
\ already there as cleared, and records the FITID on it so that a
\ later import recognises it immediately.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Home:Rates    | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense | false       |
And transactions are : ImportedTransaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee             | CheckNo | Destination         | Amount   | Cleared   | FitId |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-02 | County Tax Office | 2041    | Expenses:Home:Rates | -1250.00 | Uncleared | none  |
And import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
When transactions imported : OfxTransactions
| TrnType | DatePosted | Amount   | FitId       | Name              | Memo | CheckNum |
| CHECK   | 2024-01-03 | -1250.00 | 20240103002 | COUNTY TAX OFFICE | none | 2041     |
Then import summary is : ImportSummary Vertical
| New       | 0 |
| Duplicate | 1 |
| Possible  | 0 |
And transactions are : ImportedTransaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee             | CheckNo | Destination         | Amount   | Cleared | FitId       |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-02 | County Tax Office | 2041    | Expenses:Home:Rates | -1250.00 | Cleared | 20240103002 |
And transaction notes are : TransactionNote
| Ref | Note            |
| T1  | CheckNumberSame |
| T1  | Uncleared       |
| T1  | FitIdAdded      |
# The date entered by hand is kept. It is the date the check was written, which
# is what the register is ordered by; the clearing date belongs to the bank.
#
# Three notes, all of them true of this one transaction: the check number is
# what recognised it, it was typed rather than downloaded, and it had no
# identifier until this import gave it one. The dates differ, which is the
# ordinary case for a check and the reason the window exists, so nothing is
# said about that.

Scenario A duplicate the payees disagree about is still a duplicate, with a note
Description The one note that has no worked example above. No payee rule covers
\ this name and the loose comparison cannot join it either --- OIL is
\ not a store number --- so the two names stand as different. The date
\ and the amount in one account are enough to say this is the same
\ transaction, so nothing is created; the note is what says the book's
\ name for it is worth a second look. See two payees are compared
\ after the rules and then loosely.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type     | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel     | Expense  | false       |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense  | false       |
And transactions are : ImportedTransaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee | Destination        | Amount | Cleared | FitId |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-15 | Shell | Expenses:Auto:Fuel | -45.00 | Cleared | none  |
And import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
When transactions imported : OfxTransactions
| TrnType | DatePosted | Amount | FitId       | Name               | Memo | CheckNum |
| DEBIT   | 2024-01-15 | -45.00 | 20240115001 | SHELL OIL 57444692 | none | none     |
Then import summary is : ImportSummary Vertical
| New       | 0 |
| Duplicate | 1 |
| Possible  | 0 |
And transactions are : ImportedTransaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee | Destination        | Amount | Cleared | FitId       |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-15 | Shell | Expenses:Auto:Fuel | -45.00 | Cleared | 20240115001 |
And transaction notes are : TransactionNote
| Ref | Note           |
| T1  | PayeeUnmatched |
| T1  | FitIdAdded     |
# Shell is kept. Twenty years of calling it Shell is what the book is for, and
# the bank's name for it is not an improvement -- see what a duplicate does to
# the transaction already there. Two notes: the payees did not agree, and the
# transaction had no identifier until this import gave it one. Nothing about
# the cleared status, because it was already cleared.

Scenario A typed transaction and a download two days apart are two transactions
Description T1 was typed, which is what Uncleared says about it. The download
\ is two days later for the same amount, and the import does not
\ join them: an amount and a date is all there is to go on, and
\ reaching across days on that much is guessing.
Details What a person would have to know to answer this is whether they
\ shopped twice, and the program cannot know it. So it does not
\ decide: the download comes in as a new transaction, and T1 is left
\ alone for the pass that pairs registers later, where the question
\ is asked with two dates from two different places behind it.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries     | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense | false       |
