Scenario A new transaction is created uncategorised and cleared
Description What the bank sends is known to have happened, so it arrives cleared.
\ The category is not known, so the other side is Uncategorized until
\ a payee rule or the user says otherwise. See Payees.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense | false       |
And import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
When transactions imported : OfxTransactions
| TrnType | DatePosted | Amount | FitId       | Name               | Memo     | CheckNum |
| DEBIT   | 2024-01-15 | -45.00 | 20240115001 | HARRIS TEETER #231 | PURCHASE | none     |
Then transactions are : ImportedTransaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee              | Destination            | Amount | Cleared | FitId       |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-15 | HARRIS TEETER #231 | Expenses:Uncategorized | -45.00 | Cleared | 20240115001 |
# The payee is the raw NAME from the file. Tidying it is the business of
# Payees, so that this file has one job.

BusinessRule A download arrives cleared and only a typed transaction is not
Description Cleared means the bank has said this happened. A download is the
\ bank saying so, so everything read out of one is cleared. A
\ transaction is uncleared only while the only record of it is that
\ somebody typed it.
Details Which makes cleared status the one thing in a book that says where a
\ transaction came from. The matching tests above used to turn on it --
\ an uncleared transaction was asked about and a cleared one was
\ matched silently -- and they do not any more: both are the same
\ transaction arriving again, and which it was is recorded as a note
\ instead of changing the answer. What cleared status is for is the
\ reconciliation and the balance the bank states, which is the job it
\ was always doing.
\
\ A download states a cleared status of its own in some formats, and it
\ is not read. Nothing a bank has sent is uncleared by the time it is
\ in the file, so a flag saying otherwise is either about something
\ else or wrong.
\
\ A QIF history is the exception, and it is not a download: it is one
\ migration of twenty years of a Quicken file, and the C field in it is
\ the only thing that says which of those invoices were ever paid.
\ Three income figures agree with Quicken to the cent because of it.
\ So a history keeps the marker it carries, which leaves the
\ transactions it brought in cleared where Quicken had cleared them and
\ uncleared where it had not. See the cleared marker rule in ImportQif,
\ and was_paid in qif_import.h for what that marker replaced.
\
\ A QIF chosen as an ordinary import is not that. It is a statement
\ that happens to be in Quicken's format -- one account, one period,
\ reviewed in the pane beside its register like any other download --
\ and it arrives cleared with the rest of them. The two cases are told
\ apart by which question asked for the file: the history is asked for
\ once, when the book is made, and nowhere else. See the new book
\ question in UserInterface.
Examples: ClearedOnArrival
| HowItArrived               | Cleared          |
| Read from an OFX download  | Cleared          |
| Read from a CSV download   | Cleared          |
| Read from a QIF statement  | Cleared          |
| Read from a QIF history    | As the file says |
| Typed into the register    | Uncleared        |
| Typed as a split           | Uncleared        |

Attributes ClearedOnArrival
| Attribute     | Type   | Default |
| HowItArrived  | String |         |
| Cleared       | String |         |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What a check number is worth, and for how long
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

BusinessRule A check number is trusted for a day and no longer
Description A check number is the strongest thing in a bank file: it is written
\ on the check itself, so the book and the download are naming the
\ same piece of paper. But only for as long as the number means what
\ it says. Check numbers are reused -- a new book of checks starts
\ where an old one left off, and over twenty years the same number
\ comes round again.
Details So a number settles a match only when the two are dated within the
\ book's check-number window of each other. Beyond that the number is
\ treated as coincidence and the weaker tests decide, which usually means
\ the transaction is created.
\
\ It asks for the amount and the payee to agree as well -- a number on
\ its own is a number, and the three together are a check -- and it is
\ asked only of a checking account. A card's reference number is not a
\ check number and nothing else in a book is paid by check.
\
\ The window is a setting on the book, a whole number of days, and it is
\ one day unless it is changed. One day is right for a book whose checks
\ are never entered before the bank reports them: nothing is outstanding,
\ so there is nothing for a number to reach back to, and a number that
\ matches anything older is more likely a number reused than a check
\ still in the post.
\
\ This is the only window inside an import. Every test below the number
\ asks about one day, so the number is the one thing that reaches further
\ than the day the bank posted, and at one day it reaches exactly one day
\ further. A book that records checks when they are written has checks
\ outstanding for a week or three, and widening the window is what makes
\ the number worth having. Recording a check when it is written is not a
\ thing this program does yet.
\
\ What happens then is the point of the rule. A number that matched while
\ the dates did not is exactly the case worth seeing: either a check
\ really did take a fortnight to clear, in which case the book now holds
\ two of it and one should be deleted, or the number came round again
\ and the import was right to create one. The program cannot tell those
\ apart, so it does not try: it writes a note against the import and
\ shows the notes when the import finishes.
\
\ Nothing is silently reconciled on a number alone, and nothing is
\ silently left out. A note that nobody reads costs a line on a screen;
\ a check quietly matched to the wrong year costs an afternoon with a
\ statement.
\ Every row here is at the default window of one day.
Examples: CheckNumberWindow
| DaysApart | NumberMatches | Duplicate | NoteWritten | Notes                                              |
| 0         | Yes           | Yes     | No          | The number settles it                              |
| 1         | Yes           | Yes     | No          | Still within the day                               |
| 2         | Yes           | No      | Yes         | The number is stale and nothing weaker reaches     |
| 16        | Yes           | No      | Yes         | Nothing reaches; created, and the note says why    |
| 16        | No            | No      | No          | A different number, and nothing else reaches       |

Attributes CheckNumberWindow
Description Whether a check number settles a match, by how far apart the two are
\ dated, and whether the import wrote a note about it.
Details Duplicate is the disposition, not the reason for it. The row two days
\ apart is created rather than matched, because at the default window
\ nothing reaches two days: the number is past its day and the amount
\ never reached beyond the day at all. The note is written all the same,
\ because the number said one check and the dates said another and that
\ is what a person needs to see -- a created transaction is the right
\ answer and still worth being told about.
| Attribute     | Type    | Default |
| DaysApart     | Integer | 0       |
| NumberMatches | Text    | Yes     |
| Duplicate     | Text    | No      |
| NoteWritten   | Text    | No      |
| Notes         | String  | none    |

Scenario A check number that matches too long afterwards does not settle it
Description The same number sixteen days later. The number alone is not enough
\ to reconcile a check across that gap, so the transaction is created
\ and a note says what was seen -- leaving a person to decide whether
\ it is the same check or the number come round again.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Home:Rates    | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense | false       |
And transactions are : ImportedTransaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee             | CheckNo | Destination         | Amount   | Cleared   | FitId |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-02 | County Tax Office | 2041    | Expenses:Home:Rates | -1250.00 | Uncleared | none  |
And import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
When transactions imported : OfxTransactions
| TrnType | DatePosted | Amount   | FitId       | Name              | Memo | CheckNum |
| CHECK   | 2024-01-18 | -1250.00 | 20240118002 | COUNTY TAX OFFICE | none | 2041     |
Then import summary is : ImportSummary Vertical
| New      | 1 |
And import notes are : ImportNote
| Kind             | Detail                                                       |
| CheckNumberStale | Check 2041 is dated 2024-01-02 here and 2024-01-18 in the file |
# The one entered by hand is untouched and uncleared; the downloaded one is a
# new transaction. The note is what tells a person to look, because only a
# person knows whether that check took a fortnight or whether the numbers came
# round again.

Attributes ImportNote
Description Something the import saw and could not decide, kept against the
\ import and shown when it finishes. Modelled on MigrationNote, which
\ does the same job for a QIF.
| Attribute | Type | Default |
| Kind      | Text |         |
| Detail    | Text |         |

Scenario A wider check-number window reaches a check that took a fortnight
Description The same check sixteen days later, in a book whose window is thirty
\ days. The number settles it, so the one entered by hand is cleared
\ rather than a second one created -- which is what a book that
\ records checks when they are written needs.
Given the book setting is : BookSetting Vertical
| CheckWindowDays | 30 |
And the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Home:Rates    | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense | false       |
And transactions are : ImportedTransaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee             | CheckNo | Destination         | Amount   | Cleared   | FitId |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-02 | County Tax Office | 2041    | Expenses:Home:Rates | -1250.00 | Uncleared | none  |
And import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
When transactions imported : OfxTransactions
| TrnType | DatePosted | Amount   | FitId       | Name              | Memo | CheckNum |
| CHECK   | 2024-01-18 | -1250.00 | 20240118002 | COUNTY TAX OFFICE | none | 2041     |
Then import summary is : ImportSummary Vertical
| New       | 0 |
| Duplicate | 1 |
And import notes are : ImportNote
| Kind | Detail |
# No note: inside the window the number is trusted, so there is nothing a
# person needs to decide. This is the one thing no other test can do -- every
# one of them wants the dates to agree, and these are sixteen days apart.

Attributes BookSetting
Description A setting the book carries, in its own single row. Defaulted here as
\ it is defaulted on a new book. HideBefore is a date or the word none,
\ so Text rather than Date: a book that hides nothing has no date to
\ state, and a Date attribute would have to default to one.
| Attribute             | Type    | Default |
| CheckWindowDays       | Integer | 1       |
| SetClearedOnCsvImport | Boolean | true    |
| FiscalYearStart       | Integer | 1       |
| HideBefore            | Text    | none    |

Scenario The notes are shown when the import finishes
Description An import that wrote notes says so at the end, with the notes. A
\ note nobody is shown is a note nobody acts on, and the whole reason
\ for writing one is that the program could not decide.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense | false       |
And transactions are : ImportedTransaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee   | CheckNo | Destination            | Amount  | Cleared   | FitId |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2024-03-01 | Plumber | 3100    | Expenses:Uncategorized | -420.00 | Uncleared | none  |
And import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
When transactions imported : OfxTransactions
| TrnType | DatePosted | Amount  | FitId | Name    | Memo | CheckNum |
| CHECK   | 2024-03-20 | -420.00 | F900  | PLUMBER | none | 3100     |
Then import finishes with : ImportCompletion Vertical
| NoteCount | 1                                     |
| Summary   | 1 new, 1 note to look at              |

Attributes ImportCompletion
Description What the import says when it is done.
| Attribute | Type    | Default |
| NoteCount | Integer | 0       |
| Summary   | Text    | none    |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Resolving what the import was not sure about
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

BusinessRule A possible duplicate is the only disposition that waits
Description The other three settle themselves. A new transaction is created, a
\ duplicate clears the one already in the book and creates nothing, a
\ completed payment fills in the half that was waiting. A possible
\ duplicate is the case the program cannot settle, so it is the one
\ that asks, and nothing happens to it until it is answered.
Details One thing produces it: the file and the book disagreeing about how
\ many. Where the same date and amount is in the file twice and in the
\ book once, or in the file once and in the book twice, nothing says
\ which row goes with which transaction. The counts are the whole of
\ the evidence and they do not add up.
\
\ What a person needs to know to answer it is whether they shopped
\ twice, and the program cannot know that. So it does not decide. Every
\ row of the disagreeing set is offered, and each is answered on its
\ own: this one is the transaction already there, or this one is
\ another the book does not have.
\
\ An import left unanswered changes nothing, which is what makes it
\ safe to put it down and come back to it. A possible duplicate quietly
\ created would double a transaction and one quietly discarded would
\ lose it. Over twenty years both happen, and the second is worse: an
\ extra row is seen and deleted, a missing one is found a year later or
\ never.
Examples: PossibleAnswer
| Answer     | TheOneAlreadyThere                       | TheDownloadedRow            |
| TheSame    | Cleared, and stamped with the identifier | Nothing is created          |
| Different  | Nothing                                  | A transaction is created    |
| Unanswered | Nothing                                  | Nothing; it is asked again  |

Attributes PossibleAnswer
| Attribute          | Type   | Default |
| Answer             | String |         |
| TheOneAlreadyThere | String |         |
| TheDownloadedRow   | String |         |

Scenario Answering that one of two offered rows is the transaction already there
Description Two coffees, or one coffee entered by hand and read back. The file
\ has the same amount twice on the same day and the book has it once,
\ so the counts disagree and both rows are offered. Answering says
\ which is which: F003 is the one already there, F004 is the second
\ visit.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries     | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense | false       |
And transactions are : ImportedTransaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee         | Destination        | Amount | Cleared   | FitId |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2026-02-05 | Harris Teeter | Expenses:Groceries | -88.20 | Uncleared | none  |
And import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
When transactions imported : OfxTransactions
| TrnType | DatePosted | Amount | FitId | Name          | Memo | CheckNum |
| DEBIT   | 2026-02-05 | -88.20 | F003  | HARRIS TEETER | none | none     |
| DEBIT   | 2026-02-05 | -88.20 | F004  | HARRIS TEETER | none | none     |
Then import summary is : ImportSummary Vertical
| Possible | 2 |
When the possible duplicate is answered : PossibleResolution Vertical
| FitId  | F003    |
| Answer | TheSame |
And the possible duplicate is answered : PossibleResolution Vertical
| FitId  | F004      |
| Answer | Different |
Then transactions are : ImportedTransaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee         | Destination            | Amount | Cleared | FitId |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2026-02-05 | Harris Teeter | Expenses:Groceries     | -88.20 | Cleared | F003  |
| T2  | Assets:Checking | 2026-02-05 | HARRIS TEETER | Expenses:Uncategorized | -88.20 | Cleared | F004  |
# T1 keeps the payee and the category it was given and gains the identifier it
# never had, so reading the same file again settles on test one and asks
# nothing. T2 is created like any new row, cleared because it came from the
# bank and uncategorised until a payee rule or the user says otherwise.

Attributes PossibleResolution
Description Which offered row is being answered, and what the answer is.
| Attribute | Type  | Default |
| FitId     | FitId |         |
| Answer    | Text  | TheSame |

Scenario Answering that both offered rows are different transactions
Description Three genuine visits to one shop on one day for one amount: one
\ typed, two downloaded, and the bank is describing two the book does
\ not have.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries     | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense | false       |
And transactions are : ImportedTransaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee         | Destination        | Amount | Cleared   | FitId |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2026-02-05 | Harris Teeter | Expenses:Groceries | -88.20 | Uncleared | none  |
And import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
When transactions imported : OfxTransactions
| TrnType | DatePosted | Amount | FitId | Name          | Memo | CheckNum |
| DEBIT   | 2026-02-05 | -88.20 | F003  | HARRIS TEETER | none | none     |
| DEBIT   | 2026-02-05 | -88.20 | F004  | HARRIS TEETER | none | none     |
And the possible duplicate is answered : PossibleResolution Vertical
| FitId  | F003      |
| Answer | Different |
And the possible duplicate is answered : PossibleResolution Vertical
| FitId  | F004      |
| Answer | Different |
Then transactions are : ImportedTransaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee         | Destination            | Amount | Cleared   | FitId |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2026-02-05 | Harris Teeter | Expenses:Groceries     | -88.20 | Uncleared | none  |
| T2  | Assets:Checking | 2026-02-05 | HARRIS TEETER | Expenses:Uncategorized | -88.20 | Cleared   | F003  |
| T3  | Assets:Checking | 2026-02-05 | HARRIS TEETER | Expenses:Uncategorized | -88.20 | Cleared   | F004  |
# T1 is left exactly as it was typed, uncleared and unstamped: answering
# Different says the download is not it, which is not a statement about T1 at
# all. The identifiers are on the new ones only, so a later file naming F003 is
# a duplicate of the second visit and nothing else.

Scenario An unanswered possible duplicate changes nothing and is asked again
Description Which is what makes it safe to put an import down and come back to
\ it. Nothing is created, nothing is stamped and nothing is cleared,
\ so reading the file again reaches the same question.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries     | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense | false       |
And transactions are : ImportedTransaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee         | Destination        | Amount | Cleared   | FitId |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2026-02-05 | Harris Teeter | Expenses:Groceries | -88.20 | Uncleared | none  |
And import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
When transactions imported : OfxTransactions
| TrnType | DatePosted | Amount | FitId | Name          | Memo | CheckNum |
| DEBIT   | 2026-02-05 | -88.20 | F003  | HARRIS TEETER | none | none     |
| DEBIT   | 2026-02-05 | -88.20 | F004  | HARRIS TEETER | none | none     |
Then import summary is : ImportSummary Vertical
| Possible | 2 |
And transactions are : ImportedTransaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee         | Destination        | Amount | Cleared   | FitId |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2026-02-05 | Harris Teeter | Expenses:Groceries | -88.20 | Uncleared | none  |
# Unchanged, and neither identifier is anywhere, so the same file asks the same
# question. An import that was not answered is not a decision.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Two downloaded transactions that are one transfer
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Description Importing a file for each account brings in both halves of a
\ transfer as two separate transactions. Recognising them as one is a
\ different problem from the matching above, because neither half is
\ already in the book: both are new in the same import.
Details
\ It is never done without asking. Two accounts at one bank moving the
\ same round number on the same day is exactly what a genuine pair of
\ separate payments looks like, and merging those would lose one of them.
\ So a pair is proposed and the user approves it, and an approval can be
\ remembered so that the next month's import proposes the same thing with
\ the reasoning already made.

DataType TransferCandidateState
Description How far a proposed pairing has got.
Examples: EnumerationValues
| Value     | Notes                                                       |
| Proposed  | Found, and waiting to be approved or rejected               |
| Approved  | Made into one transfer                                      |
| Rejected  | Left as two transactions, each with an Uncategorized side   |
| Automatic | Approved without asking, by a remembered rule               |

Attributes TransferCandidate
Description One proposed pairing, with everything the user needs to judge it.
| Attribute   | Type                   | Default    |
| FromAccount | AccountPath            |            |
| FromDate    | Date                   | 2026-01-01 |
| FromName    | Text                   | none       |
| FromFitId   | FitId                  | none       |
| ToAccount   | AccountPath            |            |
| ToDate      | Date                   | 2026-01-01 |
| ToName      | Text                   | none       |
| ToFitId     | FitId                  | none       |
| Amount      | Money                  | 0.00       |
| DaysApart   | Integer                | 0          |
| State       | TransferCandidateState | Proposed   |

BusinessRule When two new transactions are proposed as one transfer
Description All of these, and then it is proposed rather than done.
Details The window is two days, wider than the same day a download is
\ allowed to reach when it is matched against a transaction already in
\ the book. The two rules are answering different questions. There the
\ evidence is an amount and a date, and a date somebody typed is the day
\ they paid rather than the day the bank posted; here both halves are
\ electronic and both dates are the bank's own, so two days is the lag
\ between two postings rather than a guess about one. Even so every
\ extra day of window is another chance to pair two payments that were
\ never one transfer.
\ Borne out by the three files in testdata: of the seven pairs in them,
\ five are on the same day and the widest is the credit card autopay of
\ 3 August, which posted to the card on the 1st. Nothing is further
\ apart than two days.
Examples: TransferCandidateRule
| Test | Condition                                                            |
| 1    | Neither has been claimed by this import already                       |
| 2    | They are in two different accounts, and both accounts are real        |
| 3    | The amounts are equal and opposite                                    |
| 4    | The dates are within two days                                         |
| 5    | Neither has already been proposed as half of another pair             |

BusinessRule A payment is completed by whichever import arrives second
Description A payment from a bank account to a credit card is downloaded twice,
\ once from each side, and the two downloads usually happen on
\ different days in either order. Whichever arrives first records the
\ payment as an ordinary row. Whichever arrives second finds that
\ transaction, asks whether the two are the same payment, and on being
\ told they are completes it rather than recording the payment again.
Details So the two halves of a transfer do not have to arrive together, which
\ the first version of this rule required and which no real month looks
\ like. The side that arrives second brings two things: the account, which
\ replaces the Uncategorized other side the first row was given, and a row
\ of its own -- a transaction is one account's own download, so the second
\ half is a transaction in its own right, not a rewrite of the first, and
\ it carries its own identifier rather than a second one squeezed onto the
\ first's.
\
\ Each side's payee is whatever its own file called it. A bank calls it
\ a payment to the card; a card calls it a payment received. The first
\ side's own payee is never touched by the second arriving -- there is
\ nothing to overwrite, since each is its own transaction.
\
\ Matching needs the amount equal and opposite and the dates within the
\ transfer window, and that is the whole of the evidence. It does not
\ shortlist by payee first, which an earlier version of this rule did.
\ The two sides of a transfer are called different things by the two
\ banks that wrote them, and requiring a payee recorded against the
\ account in advance meant that a payment nobody had set that up for
\ was never found at all -- which is the case that sent this back to be
\ rewritten. Weaker evidence is exactly why nothing is paired without
\ being asked: see the rule below.
Examples: PaymentCompletion
| WhichArrivesFirst | WhatItRecords                            | WhatTheSecondDoes                 |
| The bank          | The payment, other side Uncategorized    | Asks, then names the card on it   |
| The card          | The payment, other side Uncategorized    | Asks, then names the bank on it   |
| Only one ever     | The payment, other side Uncategorized    | Nothing; it is already a true row |

Attributes PaymentCompletion
| Attribute         | Type   | Default |
| WhichArrivesFirst | String |         |
| WhatItRecords     | String |         |
| WhatTheSecondDoes | String |         |

BusinessRule A half already in the book is found on the amount and the date
Description Every account a register can be opened on is searched, not a
\ shortlist. Equal and opposite, within the window, in some other
\ account: that is a candidate, and a candidate is put to the user
\ rather than acted on.
Details The amount and the date are weaker evidence than a payee recorded
\ against the account in advance would have been, and the dialog is what
\ makes that safe. It names both halves, both accounts and the gap in
\ days, and asks whether they are the same payment. Two separate
\ payments of the same amount in the same week are exactly what this
\ would otherwise merge, and nobody but the person who made them can
\ tell. So the search is as wide as it can be and the answer is always
\ asked for, which is the opposite trade from the one the payee rule
\ made: it searched narrowly so that it could act quietly, and the cost
\ was every payment it never found.
\
\ The first side to arrive is therefore an ordinary row with an
\ Uncategorized other side, like anything else whose category is not
\ known. It is not held in a staging account waiting for a partner. It
\ is a finished transaction that may later be told what the other side
\ of it was, and if no partner ever arrives it is already right.
\
\ Several halves already here can match one downloaded row. Each pairing
\ is then offered in its own right rather than one of them being picked,
\ and approving one takes the rest off the table, because the row they
\ were about is spoken for.
Examples: AlreadyHereSearch
| WhatTheSearchFinds         | WhatHappens                                   |
| One half, in another account | Asked, naming that account and the gap       |
| Several halves             | Each asked, until one of them is approved     |
| None                       | Nothing asked; an ordinary new row            |
| A half in the same account | Not a candidate; a transfer has two accounts  |

Attributes AlreadyHereSearch
| Attribute          | Type   | Default |
| WhatTheSearchFinds | String |         |
| WhatHappens        | String |         |

Scenario A card payment downloaded from the bank is an ordinary row until the card arrives
Description The bank is read first. It knows the money left and it does not know
\ which card was paid, because the card has not been read yet -- so
\ there is nothing in the book to pair it with and nothing to ask.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type       | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking   | false       |
| Liabilities:Chase Visa | CreditCard | false       |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense    | false       |
And import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
When transactions imported : OfxTransactions
| TrnType | DatePosted | Amount   | FitId  | Name      | Memo | CheckNum |
| XFER    | 2026-04-10 | -1200.00 | BNK-77 | CHASEBANK | none | none     |
Then transactions are : ImportedTransaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee     | Destination            | Amount   | Cleared | FitId  |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2026-04-10 | CHASEBANK | Expenses:Uncategorized | -1200.00 | Cleared | BNK-77 |
And transfer candidates are : TransferCandidate
| FromAccount | FromDate | ToAccount | ToDate | Amount | DaysApart | State |
# No rows: the card has not been read, so there is no other half to find.
# Uncategorized, and no staging account: a payment whose other side is not
# known yet is not a different kind of thing from a payment whose category is
# not known yet, and it is a finished transaction either way. An XFER type in
# the file does not make something a transfer; two halves do.

BusinessRule The date windows and why they differ
Description Written down together because having four windows in one program
\ invites someone to unify them, and unifying them breaks one case or
\ another. Each row is a different question about two dates, and the
\ window follows from whose dates they are.
Details Nearly all of this is same-day, and deliberately so. While a row is
\ being identified the only window is the check-number one, where a
\ number says the two records are of one payment and the days between
\ them are the bank's lag and nothing else.
\
\ Everywhere else inside the import the dates agree or they do not, and
\ it makes no difference how the transaction already there was come by.
\ Two records of one posting from one bank agree about the day, so a
\ day's difference is a difference and not a lag. A transaction somebody
\ typed carries their date rather than the bank's, and an amount and a
\ date is too little evidence to reach across days on -- so nothing
\ reaches, and what the import has instead of a window is the count: as
\ many as the book has is a duplicate, any other number is a question.
\
\ The last row is the window that used to be inside the import and is
\ not any more. Pairing a transaction in one register against one in
\ another is asked of transactions after the import has been accepted,
\ never of rows while each one is being judged, and it has a window
\ because its two dates come from two different places.
Examples: DateWindow
| Rule                                              | Window   | Why                                        |
| A download against a check already in the book    | 1 day    | The number carries it; see the check rule  |
| A download against anything else in the book      | Same day | Two records of one posting agree about the day, and an amount and a date cannot reach further |
| Two new downloads proposed as one transfer        | 2 days   | Both halves are electronic and near        |
| One register's transaction against another's      | 5 days   | Asked after the import, of two books' own dates |

Attributes DateWindow
| Attribute | Type   | Default |
| Rule      | String |         |
| Window    | String |         |
| Why       | String |         |

Attributes TransferCandidateRule
| Attribute | Type    | Default |
| Test      | Integer | 0       |
| Condition | String  |         |

